pokemon one of the most successful  franchises in the history of video games  spawning a massively successful trading  card game and anime blockbuster films  toys clothing and so much more while the  main entries in this beloved series have  remained relatively faithful to their  tried and true formula they've still  seen new ideas that have helped them  influence future games and with that  let's take a look at my top 10 pokemon  games to play  in no particular order  i still remember to this day when i  first received my nintendo power with a  feature about pokemon and a mini comic  of the anime featuring ash meeting  pikachu before even playing the game  staring over the maps of the world of  pokemon i was hooked something about  this world just felt magical enter  pokemon red and blue a game invented by  satoshi tajiri thanks to his combined  interest in collecting bugs as a child  the link capability of game boys  allowing players to trade data and  wanting to be able to trade rare items  in dragon quest with his friends who'd  found rare items he hadn't the game  truly felt like it was made with love  and care with it having its own  fleshed-out universe  it was a world where people worked  together with pokemon who would help  them travel or destroy obstacles in the  way as well as fight and little nuances  like magikarp being the weakest joke of  a pokemon evolving into the intimidating  and strong gyrados were just the right  kind of quirks to bring the world to  life and while other pokemon games  iterated on pokemon and you could argue  to an extent made it better this game  set the stage for all of the pokemon  games to come and was so good at what it  did that even today the formula hasn't  strayed too far not only that but i  would argue one of the things about red  and blue i personally loved was that it  felt more feasible to become a true  pokemon master while the world of  pokemon is more vibrant than ever today  with 898 pokemon red and blue was a more  simple time with only 150 pokemon total  or 151 if you count mew or 152 if you  also count missing though and so long as  you traded with friends you could  actually collect every single pokemon in  this singular game even mew who you can  actually get thanks to a glitch this  will be contentious but for myself this  is something i actually really prefer  about red and blue and that feeling of  finally catching taurus my 150th pokemon  in the nightmare that was safari's own  is something i'll never forget  at his heart pokemon was a game about  collecting and for the avid collector  like myself that act alone made it worth  playing but what if you took that idea  and instead made it so you were  essentially collecting photos that's  what pokemon snap did the game put you  on a relatively linear track where you  were tasked with taking photos of the  various pokemon on your course your goal  was simple enough take photos of every  pokemon but the game made it so many of  them would only appear within certain  conditions you could trigger by  interacting with the course this created  a puzzle element to the game where you  were tasked with figuring out how to  make a pokemon act in more interesting  ways for better photos how to make  certain pokemon appear in the first  place and how to open up new paths that  would take you to new courses while the  game is unfortunately rather short it  goes to show how a simple concept  executed well can be far more fun than  many complex games the simple act of  solving puzzles and collecting photos of  the various cute and or cool pokemon in  clever ways was all the game needed to  be unique and memorable for an entire  generation who played games on the n64  and thanks to its popularity the game  finally saw a sequel released in 2021  which was similarly well received  love it or hate it 2016 was dominated by  the cultural force that was pokemon go  pokemon go was so huge it even dominated  mainstream news with countless stories  about people jaywalking or going to  restricted areas in order to catch  pokemon i even remember everyone at the  company i worked for at the time taking  extended  uh  coffee breaks in order to just walk  around and catch pokemon together or  become gym leaders while 2016's a  massive surge in pokemon go's success  was a bit of a flash in the pan as the  features were at that time pretty  limited a faithful core audience  remained and still remains to this day  with the game receiving several updates  that have completely enhanced the  experience whether this be quality of  life updates to make the general grind  more enjoyable or adding much wanted and  needed features like trading pokemon  sending friends gifts raid battles and  honestly a lot more while pokemon go was  very much initially its own one-off  thing it actually had an influence on  the main series pokemon let's go pikachu  and let's go eevee use the switch's  joy-con to catch pokemon in a similar  manner to how you catch pokemon in  pokemon go  released on game boy color pokemon gold  and silver brought back all of the  original pokemon from red and blue with  new enhanced looks while adding another  100 pokemon to the mix like red and blue  it was heavily reliant on trading  mechanics with gold and silver offering  slightly different collections to  encourage trainers to engage with their  community and friends but far from a  simple retreat pokemon gold and silver  introduced almost as many new mechanics  and features to the series as the  original those included a day night  cycle and weekly calendar so certain  pokemon would only appear at certain  times of day or on specific days of the  week pokemon gained the ability to hold  items that would restore health or  improve passive stats gold and silver  introduced new shiny pokemon extremely  rare variants with special coloring and  enhanced stats who for all of my years  playing pokemon i still haven't caught a  single one and i kind of secretly hate  the game for it gold and silver also  added not one but two new pokemon types  steel and dark to counter poison and  psychic pokemon respectively and they  introduced pokemon breeding which let  players pair their favorite monsters to  produce a new egg that would hatch into  pokemon that inherited stat bonuses from  its parents in addition to the eight  gyms in the jodo region gold and silver  actually took players back to the  original kanto setting of red and blue  for a second set of batches effectively  making the experience feel like two  games in one pokemon gold and silver are  still considered by many fans to be a  high water mark for this series like red  and blue gold and silver received an  enhanced version called pokemon crystal  while it was mostly similar it let  players pick their character's gender  for the first time add a new start of  battle animations and introduce the  battle tower for a gauntlet of pokemon  bouts  while there was pokemon stadium on the  n64 which was much beloved at the time  for allowing players to transfer and  battle their pokemon they collected from  red blue and yellow but now in 3d it was  essentially a glorified version of the  tournament and fighting features already  present in those games but now in 3d  however pokken tournament is the first  full-fledged 3d pokemon fighting game  with its own fighting game mechanics  this 3d fighter takes elements from  other fighting games with 3d free  roaming similar to powerstone but also  2d fighting incorporated as well like  any good fighting game the pokemon here  have their own unique move sets and  styles meaning you'll be able to find  one that works for you and a decent  balance of play types as well as depth  and while pokken tournament obviously  came out first i'm giving pokken  tournament dx the nod here as it  includes 5 more characters and a bunch  of new modes like 3v3 fighting all of  that means that this switch game is the  ultimate edition of pokken tournament  this might be a contested entry into the  list and i think it really depends on if  you like fighting games or not for my  part while the game's roots will always  be my favorite i think it's cool to see  the series branch out and do new things  with its wonderful world  the first entries on the 3ds pokemon x  and y were released in 2013 but remained  popular for a long time afterward due to  their sheer quality game balance and  wealth of features these entries were  the first to use a fully 3d presentation  and introduced the new fairy pokemon  type to help counterbalance the powerful  dragon type this generation also  introduced the new mega evolutions which  would let fully evolved pokemon  temporarily take on a special new form  and on top of all of that the games  still peppered in new content and  quality of life features like sky  battles horde battles and a  tamagotchi-like mode called pokemon on  me while they stuck to tradition like  many other pokemon games of its era x  and y were just well crafted enough that  fans didn't mind too much  the game boy this brought the third  generation of pokemon games ruby and  sapphire while at this point pokemon was  starting to see a similar formula  forming in a new truckload of monsters  to collect a new story and some new  features that's not necessarily a bad  thing when it's done well as ruby and  sapphire was released to the game boy  advance which was far more advanced than  the game boy color that meant the game  looked much more vibrant than silver and  gold on top of that pokemon ruby and  sapphire introduced double battles that  used two pokemon at a time these  versions brought about pokemon abilities  for further strategic customization  plus you could link up with up to four  players at a time instead of only two  the second nintendo 3ds pokemon entries  felt notably different from a lot of  their predecessors and this could be in  part because of the new tropical setting  based loosely on hawaii called alola the  environment and the look of the  characters was vastly different than  anything that came before and as someone  who absolutely loves hawaii and tropical  locations this honestly really clicked  with me while it might not matter for  everyone this shift in environment and  attitude was enough to really interest  me and bring me back into a series that  as much as i love it can at times feel  repetitive sun and moon included a few  new features as well like an increased  level of character customization it also  brought about new alolan forms of  pokemon regional variants of classic  faves that according to the lore evolve  differently depending on their  environment and if you don't love the  new version of executor i don't know  what to say because it is amazing this  idea of regional variance ended up being  carried forward to the subsequent sword  and shield  pokemon black and white's plot is  anything but black and white  i throw the dad jokes in when i can you  see while pokemon has always had a  kid-friendly vibe and a magical world at  the end of the day you're kinda  sorta using your pokemon for what are  essentially dog fights and black and  white finally address that issue the  main antagonist of the game is team  plasma whose leader n sees pokemon as  friends rather than tools and wants to  liberate them from their trainers who he  believes is oppressing them and while  the moral of the story ended up being  that pokemon like being used as tools  and fighting machines you know it was  still an interesting question for a  little bit pokemon black and white  included new features like a changing of  the seasons that would open up new areas  and impact the look of a couple of  pokemon themselves you could also take  part in triple battles with a team of  three pokemon allowing for some more  complex combat tactics like combination  moves most importantly the game featured  my boy ashwat or as i lovingly named him  bubble magoo although while duwatt is  pretty cool i'm not sure how i feel  about samurai  to be honest while i absolutely loved  pokemon blue and always enjoyed the  pokemon games since pokemon's fourth  generation in pokemon diamond and pearl  on the nintendo ds was the first one i  truly truly got absorbed into after blue  while the overall core gameplay changes  were somewhat minimal i really enjoyed  the game taking full advantage of the  nintendo ds's hardware notably diamond  and pearl had the first appearance of  the global tradestation or gts meaning  that by taking advantage of the ds's  wi-fi connection players from all over  the world could connect and trade their  pokemon and you would get plenty of  pokemon with super weird names that  weren't nearly as cool as bubble magoo  as pokemon diamond and pearl utilized  the nintendo ds hardware you had a  poketech on your bottom screen which was  essentially a smartwatch with various  applications you could earn throughout  the game like a friendship checker or  barry searcher i think outside of  nostalgia and what game you played first  for some your favorite pokemon game  might also be based around the pokemon  designs within the game and personally i  feel like gen 4 has some of the stronger  designs i'm one of those players who  gets super attached to my favorite  pokemon and as someone who likes weasels  and ferrets weasel and floatzel were  absolutely awesome also lest we forget  this was the pokemon that driftloon came  from drifloon who were known for and i  quote from sun and moon stories go that  it grabs the hands of small children and  drags them away to the afterlife it  dislikes heavy children if for some  reason its body bursts its soul spills  out with a screaming sound  so there you have the 10 best pokemon  games to play  according to myself i really believe  this is going to be deeply personal  based on your own experiences  preferences and nostalgia so let me know  what you think are the 10 best pokemon  games and honestly if you think i missed  something or sword and shield deserve to  spot feel free to tell me why i'm wrong  in the comments below and i'll see you  guys next time  [Music]  you

